e, Italy", "context": "Gaius Prastina Messalinus's senatorial career and consulship were centered in Rome, the political heart of the Roman Empire."}, {"sourceSlug": "roman-imperial-administration", "sourceName": "Roman Imperial Administration", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "gaius-prastina-messalinus", "targetName": "Gaius Prastina Messalinus", "context": "Gaius Prastina Messalinus was a component of the intricate Roman Imperial Administration, holding a key position as consul."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Political center"}, {"name": "Roman Empire", "role": "Governing body"}, {"name": "Latium, Italy", "role": "Historical region"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Gaius Prastina Messalinus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "gaius-prastina-messalinus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1239662", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaius_Prastina_Messalinus"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 4, "significanceNarrative": "Gaius Prastina Messalinus's consulship in 147 CE highlights the stability of the Antonine administration and the effective functioning of the Roman senatorial system during a period of imperial peak. While not a transformative figure, his career provides a concrete example of elite governance that sustained the Pax Romana.", "significanceCategory": "local"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-05T18:44:28Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42081943119", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Gaius Prastina Messalinus was a Roman senator, active during the reign of Antoninus Pius. He was consul in the year 147 with Lucius Annius Largus as his colleague. Messalinus is known entirely from inscriptions.", "newValue": "Gaius Prastina Messalinus, a prominent Roman senator, achieved the esteemed ordinary consulship in 147 CE, a significant milestone in the Roman Republic and Empire's political structure. His career, though largely reconstructed from fragmentary epigraphic evidence, illuminates the mechanisms of elite advancement and imperial administration during the Antonine Age, a period often lauded as the Pax Romana's zenith. As a colleague to Lucius Annius Largus in the consulship, Messalinus served at the heart of Roman governance under Emperor Antoninus Pius, a ruler celebrated for his stable and prosperous reign that saw the empire reach its greatest territorial extent and enjoy relative peace.

Messalinus's attainment of the ordinary consulship, an office that lent its name to the year and carried immense prestige, signifies a successful progression through the *cursus honorum*. While the specifics of his earlier magistracies, such as quaestor, aedile, or praetor, remain largely undocumented, his ultimate ascent to the consulship indicates a career of consistent service and political acumen. The discovery of inscriptions detailing his name and title, primarily from Rome and potentially other imperial centers, provides crucial, albeit sparse, primary source material for understanding the administrative personnel of the mid-2nd century CE.

